Chapter: EngineFillGuardTest
  cites Engine chapter Renderer3D
  cites Engine chapter Scene3D
  cites Engine chapter Mesh
  cites Engine chapter Material
  cites Foreword chapter Console

 WORKS-60. A target whose depth pointer reads 0 made `r3d-target-clear` fill
 `r3d-far-depth` from address 0 upward, across the PML4 page at 0x8000, after
 which no fault could be delivered: the guest died of a triple fault naming
 nothing. The floor in `r3d-base-ok` is what refuses it.

 THIS CHAPTER'S OWN COMPLETION IS THE ASSERTION, and it is why the arms are
 shaped as they are. Run against the code before the guard, the first row does
 not fail, it takes the machine down and prints nothing at all -- so the
 evidence that the guard works is a chapter that reaches its last line. A row
 that merely compared two integers would have been just as green on a build
 that corrupts memory.

 THE CONTROL IS THE HALF THAT CAN FAIL THE OTHER WAY. A guard that refused
 EVERY base would also stop the crash and would silently blank every 3D pane
 in the desk, which no arm above would notice. So a real allocated target has
 to clear and be seen to have cleared, and its depth words are read back.

Section: Body

  efg-yn : Boolean -> Text
  efg-yn (v) = if v then "yes" else "NO"

 The dimensions are deliberately NOT degenerate. A zero-sized target would
 refuse on the count rather than the base and the row would pass without ever
 reaching the thing under test (L-VACUOUS).

  efg-w : Integer = 8
  efg-h : Integer = 4

  efg-null-depth : Text =
    let px = alloc-bytes (efg-w * efg-h * 4)
    in let st = R3dTriState { r3t-base = px, r3t-depth = 0, r3t-w = efg-w, r3t-h = efg-h, r3t-stride = efg-w }
    in let r = r3d-target-clear st 0
    in "null depth: clear answered " & integer-to-text r
       & "  refused " & efg-yn (r == r3d-refused)
       & "  and the guest is still alive to say so " & efg-yn True

  efg-null-base : Text =
    let dp = alloc-bytes (efg-w * efg-h * 4)
    in let st = R3dTriState { r3t-base = 0, r3t-depth = dp, r3t-w = efg-w, r3t-h = efg-h, r3t-stride = efg-w }
    in let r = r3d-target-clear st 0
    in "null base: clear answered " & integer-to-text r
       & "  refused " & efg-yn (r == r3d-refused)

 A LOW BUT NON-ZERO BASE IS THE ONE A `base == 0` TEST WOULD HAVE MISSED, and
 it is the shape a partially rebuilt record gives: the page tables sit at
 0x8000 and the kernel loads at 0x100000, so 0x8000 is both a plausible
 garbage value and the exact address the crash destroyed.

  efg-low-base : Text =
    let dp = alloc-bytes (efg-w * efg-h * 4)
    in let st = R3dTriState { r3t-base = 32768, r3t-depth = dp, r3t-w = efg-w, r3t-h = efg-h, r3t-stride = efg-w }
    in let r = r3d-target-clear st 0
    in "low base 0x8000: clear answered " & integer-to-text r
       & "  refused " & efg-yn (r == r3d-refused)

  efg-real : Text =
    let st = r3d-target-new efg-w efg-h 7
    in let r = r3d-target-clear st 99
    in let d0 = peek-32 (st.r3t-depth) 0
    in let dn = peek-32 (st.r3t-depth) ((efg-w * efg-h - 1) * 4)
    in let p0 = peek-32 (st.r3t-base) 0
    in "real target: clear answered " & integer-to-text r
       & "  did not refuse " & efg-yn (r /= r3d-refused)
       & "  depth[0] " & integer-to-text d0
       & "  depth[last] " & integer-to-text dn
       & "  both far " & efg-yn (d0 == r3d-far-depth & dn == r3d-far-depth)
       & "  colour[0] " & integer-to-text p0
       & "  cleared " & efg-yn (p0 == 99)

Section: Entry

  opening : [Console] Nothing =
    act
      print-line-uni efg-null-depth
      print-line-uni efg-null-base
      print-line-uni efg-low-base
      print-line-uni efg-real
      print-line-uni "engine-fill-guard: reached the end, so nothing wrote through the tables"
    end

